    "https://ap.washington.edu/ahr/position-details/?job_id=191344": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Supply Chain Management and Operations",
      "description": "Position Details - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Position Details Position Details Assistant Teaching Professor in Supply Chain Management and Operations Position Overview School / Campus / College: University of Washington, Bothell Organization: Business Title: Assistant Teaching Professor in Supply Chain Management and Operations Position Details Position Description The School of Business at the University of Washington Bothell invites applications for a teaching faculty position at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor in Supply Chain Management and Operations, with an anticipated start date of September 16, 2027. We seek applicants who demonstrate commitment to excellence in teaching and service in a diverse and inclusive academic community. The successful candidate will join our faculty on a full-time basis (100% FTE) for a 9-month academic, 3-year renewable appointment beginning September 16, 2027. The Assistant Teaching Professor position is not eligible for tenure. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in Operations Management, Business Statistics, Supply Chain Management, Business Analytics, and Artificial Intelligence; develop and update courses and curricula that integrate contemporary analytical methods, AI-enabled decision tools, and experiential learning; advise and mentor students; contribute to assessment and assurance-of-learning activities; maintain an appropriate AACSB faculty qualification status; and provide service to the School, the University, the profession, and the community. Teaching faculty at the University of Washington engage in teaching, scholarship, and service, and the successful candidate will be encouraged to pursue pedagogical innovation, applied scholarship, industry engagement, and leadership in curriculum development. Positive factors for consideration include a doctorate from a highly regarded, research-intensive U.S. institution or a comparably strong international institution; expertise spanning Supply Chain Management, Information Systems, Artificial Intelligence, and Operations; experience using contemporary analytics and AI tools in instruction; evidence of teaching effectiveness; and relevant industry or applied professional experience. The School of Business The UW Bothell School of Business is accredited by AACSB and is deeply committed to the success of its faculty in research, teaching, and service. We are well known for our undergraduate, MBA, and MS Accounting programs, and enrollment has been growing at a fast pace. Located 15 miles northeast of downtown Seattle, we work closely with industry leaders, many of whom are headquartered in the region, as well as smaller, innovative, and rapidly growing companies in software, retail, medical equipment, biotechnology, electronics, and aerospace sectors. Our students are drawn from and work in companies such as Alaska Airlines, Amazon, Amgen, Boeing, Costco, Expedia, Google, ICOS, Meta, Microsoft, Nintendo, Nvidia, Phillips, PACCAR, REI, Starbucks, Symetra, T-Mobile, Tableau, and Zillow, as well as in local, State, and Federal government. The School’s Supply Chain and Operations faculty are active scholars whose work has been published in leading journals including Journal of Operations Management, Production and Operations Management, Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, Operations Research, Decision Sciences, Journal of Supply Chain Management, and IEEE Transactions in Engineering Management, among others. The Supply Chain and Operations faculty cover courses in Statistics, Introductory Operations and Project Management, a wide range of Supply Chain Management courses, Information Systems, and recently launched courses in Business Analytics and AI, across both our undergraduate and graduate curriculum. The School of Business is an undepartmentalized School and encourages collegiality across the entire faculty. Faculty members should expect to interact with colleagues from other business disciplines. As part of UW tri-campus system, faculty members have complete access to the computing and library resources of the University of Washington. University of Washington Bothell Located in the heart of the Puget Sound region, the University of Washington Bothell is one of the most dynamic public universities in the state of Washington. UW Bothell is a student-centered campus known for engaging students through classes, team projects, and research in which students connect what they learn or create in the classroom with the broader community. This is achieved through close relationships with faculty members as teachers and mentors, and staff dedicated to student success. The academic work of the campus focuses on cross-disciplinary research, connected learning, and community engagement, and prepares students for leadership in the state of Washington and beyond. The campus offers more than 55 undergraduate and graduate degree programs in five schools. Current UW Bothell enrollment is over 6,000 students. More than 90% of undergraduates are from Washington State, and approximately 40% are first-generation college attendees. The priorities of the University’s current Strategic Plan – “Expanding Access, Achieving Excellence”, are to enhance community and campus engagement, and advance cross disciplinary teaching and scholarship. In addition to award winning faculty and staff, UW Bothell has been recognized for innovations in academic programming and support services designed to help students graduate on time and debt-free. It has also earned both recognition and awards for its work in the area of environmental sustainability and is just one of twelve institutions selected to join the launch cohort of the Postsecondary Data for Action Network (PDAN), a new national initiative co-led by the Institute for Higher Education Policy (IHEP) and the Association for Institutional Research (AIR). This selection places UW Bothell among a small group of colleges and universities collectively committed to using data in smarter, more student-centered ways. Salary The base salary range for this position will be $12,000 - $13,500 per month ($108,000 to $121,500 annually), commensurate with experience and qualifications, or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. Qualifications Applicants for this position must have earned a doctorate (or foreign equivalent) in Supply Chain Management, Operations Management, Information Systems, Management Science, Operations Research, Business Analytics, Industrial Engineering, Statistics, or a closely related field, by the date of appointment. Applicants must provide evidence of successful university-level teaching or strong potential for teaching excellence and must be able to teach across several of the following areas: Operations Management, Business Statistics, Supply Chain Management, Business Analytics, Information Systems, and Artificial Intelligence. Applicants must also provide evidence of a commitment to inclusive teaching, student mentoring, and service. Instructions Review of applications will begin upon receipt, and the position will remain open until filled. Priority will be given to those applications received by October 10, 2026. Applications will only be accepted via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/193066 . Please direct general inquiries to BSsearch@uw.edu . Applications should include the following materials: Cover Letter: A cover letter of no more than 2 pages detailing your interest in the position, relevant qualifications, and experiences Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement: A statement describing your teaching philosophy, interests, and effectiveness Teaching Portfolio: Evidence of teaching effectiveness and instructional contributions, including but not limited to student evaluations, peer evaluations, sample syllabi, other representative teaching artifacts, and teaching awards Scholarship Statement: A scholarship statement describing your scholarly activities and providing evidence of how you remain current with developments in supply chain management, operations management, information systems, business analytics, artificial intelligence, or related fields, and contribute to innovation in teaching, learning, pedagogy, curriculum development, professional practice, and other scholarly areas relevant to the discipline. The statement should focus on scholarship appropriate for teaching faculty rather than a traditional tenure-track research agenda References: Names and contact information for three references who will be prepared to submit letters of recommendation upon request Applicants should articulate how their past experiences and/or future plans in teaching, scholarship, mentoring, and/or service promote a sense of belonging and foster a welcoming and inclusive environment. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Equal Opportunity The University of Washington is committed to fostering an inclusive, respectful and welcoming community for all. As an equal opportunity employer, the University considers applicants for employment without regard to race, color, creed, religion, national origin, citizenship, sex, pregnancy, age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, disability, or veteran status consistent with UW Executive Order No. 81 . Benefits Information The University is committed to providing excellent benefits to our faculty. For a summary of available benefits, please see UWHR benefits information . Privacy Notice Your application materials may be used for employment consideration and shared in accordance with UW’s Privacy Policy. Disability Services The University of Washington is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education, and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact the Disability Services Office at 206-543-6450 or dso@uw.edu. Apply now The University of Washington is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. For help signing up, accessing your account, or submitting your application, please check out Interfolio's help and support section or get in touch via email at help@interfolio.com or phone at (877)997-8807. Title IX Notice The University of Washington does not discriminate on the basis of sex and prohibits sex discrimination and sex-based harassment in its education programs and activities, including in admission and employment. Refer to the UW policies page on the Title IX website to review (1) the University’s policies prohibiting sex- and gender-based violence, harassment, and discrimination and (2) the related procedures for addressing complaints. Inquiries about Title IX may be referred to the University’s Title IX Coordinator , the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights , or both. To report information or make a complaint about conduct that may constitute sex discrimination or sex-based harassment, submit an online Title IX report or email or call the Office of the Title IX Coordinator. Office of the Title IX Coordinator Valery Richardson, Title IX Coordinator 4311 11th Ave NE Seattle, WA 98105 206-221-7932 TitleIX@uw.edu ← Back to all positions University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
